@page "/portal/healthcheck"
@model RemoteSupport.Platform.Pages.Portal.HealthCheckModel
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Health check"; ViewData["Section"] = "portal"; var t = Model.Tenant; }
<section class="portal"><div class="wrap">
    <h1>Monthly health check</h1>
    <p class="muted">
        Chosen machines look after themselves once a month: a restore point, a clean-up, a disk optimise
        and a system file check, then a read-only inspection. The report is emailed to you, not to your
        customer.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
        <strong>Which machines:</strong> tick <em>Monthly health check</em> on a client's page and every
        machine of theirs gets it, including machines added later. A single machine can be set differently
        from that — right-click it in the console, or use its row on the client's page.
    </p>
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.Message)) { <div class="alert alert-ok">@Model.Message</div> }
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.Error)) { <div class="alert alert-bad">@Model.Error</div> }

    @if (t.HealthCheckEnabled && !t.HealthCheckReady)
    {
        <div class="alert alert-warn">The health check is on, but it cannot deliver a report yet. Fill in the mail server below.</div>
    }

    @* novalidate: the browser must never refuse this form by itself. It did, for two report addresses,
       with nothing posted and the tick still showing, so the setting looked saved and was not. *@
    <form method="post" class="form card" style="max-width:720px" novalidate>
        <h3>The check</h3>
        <label>
            <input type="checkbox" name="healthCheckEnabled" value="true" checked="@t.HealthCheckEnabled" style="width:auto">
            Switch the monthly check on for my company
        </label>
        @* Clint, 2026-10-21: he left this unticked because "Offer the monthly check to my clients" read as
           "install it on every client". It installs nothing by itself — the client's own tick does. *@
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin-top:-4px">
            This alone installs nothing. Only the clients you then tick on their own page get it, under
            Clients. Off, no machine runs it whatever is ticked, and it is removed from every machine as
            each one next connects.
        </p>

        <label>
            <input type="checkbox" name="healthCheckRunMaintenance" value="true" checked="@t.HealthCheckRunMaintenance" style="width:auto">
            Do the maintenance as well as the inspection
        </label>
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin-top:-4px">
            Unticked, the machine is only inspected and nothing is changed. Maintenance never runs without a
            restore point first, so it always skips on Windows Server, which has no System Restore.
        </p>

        <label>Day of the month</label>
        <input name="healthCheckDayOfMonth" type="number" min="0" max="28" value="@t.HealthCheckDayOfMonth">
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin-top:-4px">
            Leave at 0 and each machine picks its own day from the weekday it was installed, which spreads
            your client base over five days instead of every machine reporting on the 1st.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top:22px">Where the report goes</h3>
        <label>Send reports to</label>
        <input name="healthCheckEmailTo" type="text" inputmode="email" autocomplete="off" value="@t.HealthCheckEmailTo" placeholder="reports@@yourcompany.co.za">
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin-top:-4px">
            Your address, not your customer's. You decide what to pass on. Several addresses: separate them with ; or ,
        </p>
        @* Two different addresses do two different jobs here, and nothing on this page said so.
           Working that out meant reading a config file on a client machine. *@
        <div class="alert" style="background:#f3f6f9;border:1px solid #dfe6ec;font-size:13px;padding:10px 12px">
            The report itself is footed with your <strong>support email</strong>,
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(t.SupportEmail))
            {
                <strong>@t.SupportEmail</strong><text>, </text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>which is not set yet, </text>
            }
            so a customer who reads it knows where to write. That is set on
            <a href="/portal/branding">Branding</a> and is not the address above.
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:22px">Sending mailbox</h3>
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px">
            Held here once, on the server. It is never written into an installer and never stored on a
            customer machine, so changing it is one edit here rather than a visit to every PC.
            Use a dedicated send-only mailbox, and an app password if your provider offers one.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
            <div style="flex:2"><label>Mail server</label><input name="smtpHost" value="@t.SmtpHost" placeholder="smtp.yourprovider.co.za"></div>
            <div style="flex:1"><label>Port</label><input name="smtpPort" type="number" min="1" max="65535" value="@t.SmtpPort"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
            <div><label>Username</label><input name="smtpUser" value="@t.SmtpUser" autocomplete="off"></div>
            <div><label>Send from (optional)</label><input name="smtpFrom" value="@t.SmtpFrom" placeholder="same as username"></div>
        </div>

        <label>Password</label>
        @* The stored password is never rendered, not even as a masked value - the browser would be
           holding the real thing. A blank box leaves what is on file untouched. *@
        <input name="smtpPassword" type="password" autocomplete="new-password"
               placeholder="@(Model.HasSmtpPassword ? "a password is saved - leave blank to keep it" : "no password saved yet")">
        @if (Model.HasSmtpPassword)
        {
            <label style="margin-top:8px">
                <input type="checkbox" name="clearSmtpPassword" value="true" style="width:auto"> Remove the saved password
            </label>
        }

        <label style="margin-top:8px">
            <input type="checkbox" name="smtpUseSsl" value="true" checked="@t.SmtpUseSsl" style="width:auto"> Use encryption (leave on)
        </label>

        <div class="actions"><button class="btn btn-accent" type="submit">Save</button></div>
    </form>

    <div class="card" style="max-width:720px;margin-top:18px">
        <h3>What your customer sees</h3>
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:14px">
            Nothing, unless you send it to them. The machine does its maintenance quietly and the report
            comes to you. The report itself is written for a non-technical reader and carries your logo and
            colours, so it can be forwarded as it is.
        </p>
    </div>
</div></section>
